{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 7,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.099,
            0.104,
            0.207,
            0.121
          ],
          "content": "PROBABILITY"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.862,
            0.104,
            0.898,
            0.119
          ],
          "content": "209"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.138,
            0.902,
            0.255
          ],
          "content": "**Example 7 :** There are 40 students in Class X of a school of whom 25 are girls and 15 are boys. The class teacher has to select one student as a class representative. She writes the name of each student on a separate card, the cards being identical. Then she puts cards in a bag and stirs them thoroughly. She then draws one card from the bag. What is the probability that the name written on the card is the name of (i) a girl? (ii) a boy?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.261,
            0.826,
            0.279
          ],
          "content": "**Solution :** There are 40 students, and only one name card has to be chosen."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.118,
            0.285,
            0.557,
            0.304
          ],
          "content": "(i) The number of all possible outcomes is 40"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.311,
            0.9,
            0.329
          ],
          "content": "The number of outcomes favourable for a card with the name of a girl = 25 (Why?)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.337,
            0.716,
            0.378
          ],
          "content": "Therefore, P (card with name of $\\mathrm{a~girl)=P(Girl)=\\frac{25}{40}=\\frac{5}{8}}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.382,
            0.9,
            0.401
          ],
          "content": "(ii) The number of outcomes favourable for a card with the name of a boy = 15 (Why?)"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.407,
            0.722,
            0.449
          ],
          "content": "Therefore, P(card with name of $\\mathrm{a~body} = \\mathrm{P}(\\mathrm{B} \\mathrm{o} \\mathrm{y}) = \\frac{15}{40} \\equiv \\frac{3}{8}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.458,
            0.569,
            0.478
          ],
          "content": "**Note :** We can also determine P(Boy), by taking"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.324,
            0.491,
            0.821,
            0.532
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{P}(\\mathrm{B} \\mathrm{o} \\mathrm{y})=1-\\mathrm{P}(\\mathrm{n} \\mathrm{o} \\mathrm{t} \\mathrm{B} \\mathrm{o} \\mathrm{y})=1-\\mathrm{P}(\\mathrm{G} \\mathrm{i} \\mathrm{r} \\mathrm{l})=1-\\frac{5}{8}=\\frac{3}{8}",
          "image_source": "images/page_7_equation_10.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.54,
            0.9,
            0.579
          ],
          "content": "**Example 8 :** A box contains 3 blue, 2 white, and 4 red marbles. If a marble is drawn at random from the box, what is the probability that it will be"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.119,
            0.584,
            0.592,
            0.605
          ],
          "content": "(i) white? (ii) blue? (iii) red?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.614,
            0.901,
            0.652
          ],
          "content": "**Solution :** Saying that a marble is drawn at random is a short way of saying that all the marbles are equally likely to be drawn. Therefore, the"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.66,
            0.702,
            0.68
          ],
          "content": "number of possible outcomes  = 3 + 2 + 4 = 9 \\quad ( Why? )",
          "image_source": "images/page_7_equation_14.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.688,
            0.899,
            0.727
          ],
          "content": "Let W denote the event ‘the marble is white’, B denote the event ‘the marble is blue’ and R denote the event ‘marble is red’."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.736,
            0.668,
            0.755
          ],
          "content": "(i) The number of outcomes favourable to the event W = 2"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.098,
            0.776,
            0.135,
            0.794
          ],
          "content": "So,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.308,
            0.763,
            0.422,
            0.803
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm { P } ( \\mathbb { W } ) = { \\frac { 2 } { 9 } }",
          "image_source": "images/page_7_equation_18.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.827,
            0.189,
            0.847
          ],
          "content": "Similarly,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.278,
            0.811,
            0.69,
            0.855
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm{P}(\\mathrm{B})=\\frac{3}{9}=\\frac{1}{3} \\quad \\text { and } \\quad(\\mathrm{iii}) \\mathrm{P}(\\mathrm{R})=\\frac{4}{9}",
          "image_source": "images/page_7_equation_20.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.871,
            0.442,
            0.89
          ],
          "content": "Note that $\\mathrm{P}(\\mathrm{W}) + \\mathrm{P}(\\mathrm{B}) + \\mathrm{P}(\\mathrm{R}) = 1$"
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.435,
            0.967,
            0.563,
            0.982
          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
